WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.827 --> 00:00:08.966 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to the Africa capitals, episode two of six. First, I clear the chosen
names for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09.617 --> 00:00:17.500 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Now the western coast, where the Sahel meets the Gulf of Guinea and the
Atlantic trade has left its marks.

3
00:00:18.169 --> 00:00:20.259 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Dakar into view.

4
00:00:25.702 --> 00:00:33.713 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Dakar stands on the Cap-Vert peninsula, the westernmost point of the African
mainland, on the Atlantic.

5
00:00:34.375 --> 00:00:43.036 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The city grew from a harbour on that peninsula, and nearby Gorée Island
carries a memorial house that remembers the island’s role in the Atlantic
slave trade.

6
00:00:43.694 --> 00:00:50.776 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the peninsula city at the far west, with Gorée in the bay and the
mainland running east.

7
00:00:51.465 --> 00:00:53.845 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Banjul into view.

8
00:00:59.048 --> 00:01:05.875 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Banjul stands on an island in the mouth of the River Gambia, on the Atlantic
coast of The Gambia.

9
00:01:06.526 --> 00:01:19.622 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The country takes its name from the river that reaches the sea here; the
British founded the capital in 1816 as Bathurst, and it took the name Banjul
in 1973.

10
00:01:20.278 --> 00:01:29.961 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the capital on an island in the river mouth, one of the smallest
capitals in Africa, inside the narrow country inside Senegal.

11
00:01:30.659 --> 00:01:33.422 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Bissau into view.

12
00:01:37.683 --> 00:01:44.545 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Bissau lies on the Rio Geba, on the small Atlantic coast of Guinea-Bissau,
south of Senegal.

13
00:01:45.205 --> 00:01:54.105 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Bijagós archipelago lies just offshore, an island world of mangrove
channels and sea-turtle nesting beaches.

14
00:01:54.767 --> 00:02:04.705 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the turtle islands off the small coast below Senegal; Guinea-Bissau
and Guinea are two different countries.

15
00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:07.736 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Conakry into view.

16
00:02:12.663 --> 00:02:20.674 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Conakry stands on the Kaloum peninsula, on the Atlantic coast of Guinea, at
the mouth of the Niger River.

17
00:02:21.323 --> 00:02:33.432 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name comes from the Susu word for “beyond the waters”, describing the
crossing from the islands at the river mouth; the city began on the island of
Tombo.

18
00:02:34.092 --> 00:02:46.422 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember “beyond the waters” on the Kaloum peninsula, with more than 3,500 mm
of rain a year, one of the wettest capitals in the world.

19
00:02:47.186 --> 00:02:50.123 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Freetown into view.

20
00:02:55.221 --> 00:03:00.933 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Freetown stands where the Sierra Leone River meets the Atlantic, on the coast
of Sierra Leone.

21
00:03:01.587 --> 00:03:17.931 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It was founded in the 1790s for people freed from slavery, and the great
cotton tree where the first freed settlers are said to have given thanks in
1792 stood until a storm brought it down in 2023.

22
00:03:18.587 --> 00:03:24.729 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the freed-people settlement at the river mouth, in the small country
between Guinea and Liberia.

23
00:03:25.463 --> 00:03:28.319 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Monrovia into view.

24
00:03:33.307 --> 00:03:40.552 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Monrovia stands on a cape on the Atlantic coast of Liberia, the country
between Sierra Leone and Côte d’Ivoire.

25
00:03:41.217 --> 00:03:53.071 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It was founded in 1822 by American colonists and named after President James
Monroe; the iron pier it exported from still stands in the harbour.

26
00:03:53.722 --> 00:04:00.409 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the American colony capital on its cape, with the iron pier in the
harbour.

27
00:04:01.105 --> 00:04:03.230 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Yamoussoukro into view.

28
00:04:08.256 --> 00:04:17.463 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Yamoussoukro stands inland in the forested heart of Côte d’Ivoire, north of
the coast where Abidjan carries the country’s trade.

29
00:04:18.122 --> 00:04:31.172 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It became the national capital in 1983, and its Basilica of Our Lady of Peace,
modelled on St Peter’s in Rome, is among the largest churches in the world.

30
00:04:31.822 --> 00:04:38.730 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the inland capital with the huge basilica, well north of the lagoon
coast.

31
00:04:39.474 --> 00:04:42.585 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Accra into view.

32
00:04:47.716 --> 00:04:53.985 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Accra stands on the Gulf of Guinea, on the coast of Ghana between Côte
d’Ivoire and small Togo.

33
00:04:54.644 --> 00:05:07.485 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Kwame Nkrumah declared Ghana’s independence here in 1957, and three European
forts — Dutch, Danish and British — still stand on the old shoreline.

34
00:05:08.148 --> 00:05:16.414 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the fort coast capital, where independence was declared and where
Nkrumah’s mausoleum now stands above the square.

35
00:05:17.101 --> 00:05:19.609 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Lomé into view.

36
00:05:24.489 --> 00:05:31.652 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Lomé stands on the Gulf of Guinea, behind a narrow strip of beach, in the
southern part of Togo.

37
00:05:32.306 --> 00:05:40.572 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its Grand Marché was ruled by the “Nana Benz”, women cloth traders rich enough
to be driven in Mercedes cars.

38
00:05:41.225 --> 00:05:47.750 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the cloth-trading queens of the Grand Marché, on the beachfront
capital at the Ghana border.

39
00:05:53.437 --> 00:06:03.247 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Dakar stands on the far western peninsula, Banjul on an island in the river
mouth, and Bissau below Senegal on the turtle islands.

40
00:06:03.902 --> 00:06:17.973 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember “beyond the waters” at Conakry, the freed-people settlement at
Freetown, the American cape at Monrovia, the inland basilica at Yamoussoukro,
the fort coast at Accra and the beachfront arch at Lomé.
